  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry through Saturday.\n\nHigh pressure is centered over the area towards the Great Lakes this \nafternoon, allowing for cool temperatures and clear skies today and \ntonight. Tonight will be the coolest night of the week (and of the \nseason so far) due to radiational cooling and the position of the \nhigh pressure. Lows tonight will be in the lower to mid 40s across \nmuch of the area, with near 50F at the immediate coast. Some upper \n30s are possible in rural and typically cooler locations in the \nPiedmont. The high pressure will begin shifting offshore Wednesday \nwith temperatures warming back to near average, which are in the \nlower to mid 70s. The Eastern Shore will likely only see highs in \nthe upper 60s. The high shifts farther offshore Thursday with high \ntemperatures rising into the upper 70s to lower 80s (potentially mid \n80s well inland) ahead of another dry cold front. This cold front \ncrosses the region Thursday night with temperatures trending down to \nnear seasonal averages Friday and Saturday. Dry conditions will \ncontinue through Saturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Moisture and rain chances potentially return late this weekend \ninto early next week.\n\nBy late this weekend into early next week, there is potential that \nmoisture gets pulled northward from the Gulf, in associated with the \nprobable tropical cyclone, and pushed eastward across the region \nahead of a trough over the mid-Mississippi Valley. There is still \nmuch uncertainty on the path and timing, as the tropical depression \nhas yet to form (although will likely be named soon). However, most \nglobal models and ensemble systems depict the TC remnants merging \nwith the upper trough in vicinity of the Mid-South with accumulating \nQPF over the local area later Sunday/Monday timeframe. As would be \nexpected, there is considerable spread between the 10th and 90th \npercentile QPF in the 12z/06 ensemble guidance (EPS/GEFS/GEPS). \nTemperatures should remain near average late this weekend and early \nnext week.",
